# CSS architecture

## Tokens first, components second

A token is a decision recorded once. Raw values are decisions repeated silently.

```css
:root {
  /* spacing on a 4px grid */
  --space-1: 0.25rem;  --space-2: 0.5rem;   --space-3: 0.75rem;
  --space-4: 1rem;     --space-6: 1.5rem;   --space-8: 2rem;   --space-12: 3rem;

  /* colour as semantic roles, not values */
  --surface:            hsl(0 0% 100%);
  --surface-raised:     hsl(0 0% 98%);
  --border-subtle:      hsl(214 32% 91%);
  --text-primary:       hsl(222 47% 11%);
  --text-secondary:     hsl(215 16% 47%);
  --accent:             hsl(221 83% 53%);
  --danger:             hsl(0 72% 51%);

  /* two radii and one shadow scale, no more */
  --radius-sm: 4px;    --radius-lg: 12px;
  --shadow-raised: 0 1px 3px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.1), 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.06);

  /* fluid type and spacing */
  --step-0: clamp(1rem, 0.95rem + 0.25vw, 1.125rem);
  --step-3: clamp(1.5rem, 1.3rem + 1vw, 2rem);
}
```

Semantic colour names mean dark mode is a second value set on `:root`, not a
second stylesheet. Raw `#3b82f6` in a component is the defect that makes that
impossible.

Then enforce it, because tokens that are not enforced decay:

```sh
grep -rnoE '#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}\b' src/ --include='*.css' | grep -v ':root\|tokens'
```

Any hit outside the token file is a value that should be a token.

## Cascade layers make specificity optional

Modern CSS removes most of the reason people reached for CSS-in-JS.

```css
@layer reset, tokens, base, layout, components, utilities;
```

The order is declared once and is authoritative, so a component does not need
`!important` or a naming scheme to win over a utility.

- **Layers beat specificity.** A low-specificity rule in `components` wins over a
  high-specificity rule in `base`.
- **Never use `!important`.** If you need it, the layer order is wrong.
- Utility classes go in `utilities`, which is last, so they win by design rather
  than by selector weight.

This is the single biggest structural improvement available to an existing CSS
codebase, and it is a mechanical change.

## Cascade layers versus specificity escalation

The pattern this replaces:

```css
.card { padding: 1rem; }
.card.is-featured { padding: 2rem; }   /* 0,2,0 beats 0,1,0 by luck */
.page .card { padding: 1.5rem; }      /* 0,2,0, ties, loses on order */
.page .card.is-featured { padding: 2.5rem; }  /* the arms race continues */
```

With layers, `.is-featured` in the `components` layer wins by layer order, and
the specificity of neither selector matters.

## Contain what a component owns

Scope styles so a component cannot be changed from outside, and cannot change
anything it should not:

```css
.card { container-type: inline-size; }

/* respond to the space the component has, not the viewport */
@container (min-width: 30rem) {
  .card__body { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr auto; gap: var(--space-4); }
}
```

Container queries replace the viewport media queries that made components
unusable in a sidebar or a modal. A component that depends on the viewport is a
component that only works in one place.

## Naming that survives a rename

Use a single consistent convention and do not mix two:

```css
.card                      /* block */
.card__title               /* element */
.card--featured            /* modifier */
.card.is-loading           /* state: transient, set by code */
```

- **Modifier** changes a variant the component knows about.
- **State** is set by code and may change at any time. Do not encode it as a
  modifier; you will forget to remove it.

Prefix consistently (`pf-card` for a design-system component) so origin is
visible and specificity does not escalate.

## Pick one styling approach per project

| Approach | Use when | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tokens plus utility classes | Most product work | Class names in markup |
| CSS Modules | Component-scoped styles with plain CSS | One file per component |
| Cascade layers plus BEM | A large existing stylesheet, or vanilla | Naming discipline |
| CSS-in-JS | Genuinely dynamic styling | Runtime cost, and no cross-file cascade |

Do not mix two in one project. A half-migrated codebase is worse than either
pure state, because the rules are unpredictable.

If you are migrating, pick the target, define it in one place, and enforce it
with a lint rule rather than by review.

## Performance rules with numbers

- **Never `@import` CSS from a component.** It serialises downloads. Bundle CSS
  through the JavaScript graph or use `@layer`.
- **Prefer custom properties to preprocessor variables.** Custom properties work
  at runtime, cascade with the cascade, and can be changed per element; Sass
  variables cannot.
- Animating `transform` and `opacity` avoids layout and paint. Animating `width`,
  `height`, `top` or `left` triggers layout on every frame.
- `will-change` promotes an element permanently, which costs memory. Apply it
  just before the animation and remove it after.
- Long selector lists and universal selectors (`div div div span`) are matched
  right to left, so the rightmost selector is the one to keep simple.

## Anti-rationalisations

| Excuse | Reality |
| --- | --- |
| "Raw hex is fine for one component." | Dark mode and theming stop working. Use a semantic token. |
| "I need `!important` here." | The layer order is wrong. That is what layers are for. |
| "CSS-in-JS avoids specificity problems." | It avoids the cascade and adds a runtime and a build step. Layers solve it natively. |
| "Media queries are fine." | They respond to the viewport, so a component breaks in a sidebar. Use a container query. |
| "We will consolidate the tokens later." | Consolidating a thousand raw values is a rewrite. Generate them. |
